 relationships in the network. [Data: Entities (855, 2027, 5828)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Anatomical boundary-setting: pelvis defined relative to hip bones and hips\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes explicit boundary/landmark definitions of the pelvis using HIP BONES and HIPS. One relationship describes the pelvis as the region between the hip bones, using the hip bones as key landmarks; another states the pelvis is the area between the hips. [Data: Entities (3129, 3395, 855); Relationships (6189, 6738)].\\n\\nThis boundary framing supports downstream clinical and imaging interpretations: describing structures “within the pelvis” becomes operationally meaningful when clinicians and imaging protocols reference standardized landmarks. The dataset specifically notes that organs such as the prostate sit between the hip bones within the pelvic region (as part of the relationship description), reinforcing why the pelvis is treated as a discrete compartment in clinical descriptions. [Data: Relationships (6189); Entities (855)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Organ containment and protection links the pelvis to reproductive and abdominal organ groupings\",\"explanation\":\"The network captures two different but related associations: (1) the pelvis contains REPRODUCTIVE ORGANS and (2) the pelvis protects ABDOMINAL ORGANS. These relationships represent distinct ways the pelvis is contextualized—both as a cavity/space that contains organs and as a protective structure for organs located in the abdomen. [Data: Entities (5828, 2027, 855); Relationships (13241, 11781)].\\n\\nThe pelvis entity description reinforces these connections by describing the pelvis as containing reproductive organs and being relevant to pelvic/gynecologic concerns. The abdominal-organ link is similarly consistent with the description that the pelvis helps protect abdominal organs. [Data: Entities (855, 2027, 5828); Relationships (11781, 13241)].\"},{\"summary\":\"CT imaging is explicitly tied to pelvic evaluation and surveillance contexts\",\"explanation\":\"A key operational relationship in this community is PELVIS → CT SCAN, describing that CT scanning may include imaging of the pelvis during evaluation and surveillance for ovarian cancer, including contrast-enhanced CT of the pelvis often performed as part of combined chest/abdomen/pelvis CT protocols. [Data: Relationships (3505)].\\n\\nThis relationship positions the pelvis not only as anatomy but also as an imaging target within standardized radiology workflows. The emphasis on combined chest/abdomen/pelvis imaging indicates the pelvis is frequently included as a routine compartment in cross-sectional surveillance strategies when clinically indicated. [Data: Relationships (3505)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Pelvic MRI is represented as a dedicated modality node, linked to general MRI\",\"explanation\":\"The dataset includes an entity MRI OF THE PELVIS, described as an MRI exam focused on the pelvis to closely evaluate tissues such as the uterus, cervix, and vagina and assess possible spread. This indicates a modality specialization explicitly represented as its own node, reflecting a clinically distinct imaging protocol rather than generic MRI. [Data: Entities (4691)].\\n\\nA separate relationship connects MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING (MRI) to MRI OF THE PELVIS, framing pelvic MRI as a specific application of MRI for evaluating pelvic structures. While the general MRI entity itself is not listed among the provided entities, the relationship still encodes a hierarchy/specialization: general MRI → pelvic MRI. [Data: Relationships (10100); Entities (4691)].\"},{\"summary\":\"“Local or regional recurrence” is defined by confinement to the pelvis, highlighting pelvic compartmentalization in cancer definitions\",\"explanation\":\"The community ties the pelvis to the concept LOCAL OR REGIONAL RECURRENCE through a relationship stating that local or regional recurrence is defined as cervical cancer returning to the pelvis only. This makes